#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311} :activity/id #uuid "8b4c0c6e-e1ad-53be-980f-1125809beccc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049590 :topic/id #uuid "02cc8363-d667-570b-9679-eb87a01109a9" :topic/math-academy-id 6728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070cb125-df6a-5a6e-a3c1-eebd12e71978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377475} {:answer/id #uuid "f4013812-37ba-5064-ab55-54d4b4b78e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-2s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377476} {:answer/id #uuid "b3afdc87-a5c1-502d-b40f-2421236c8cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} {:answer/id #uuid "436278c2-b580-52a1-8e49-b07bf5e41e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+4s+13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377478} {:answer/id #uuid "665b8f05-7e39-58e8-bd72-72806cf6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{9{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} :answer-field/id #uuid "443034ac-6f38-5490-9b31-4ff0cd1c4442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c8533a8-1f90-5b54-be02-41e34f166ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345255" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1)),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+4L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+13L\\{y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the first derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=1$ and $f(t-1)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))$, so $f(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\}=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+4sY(s)+13Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4s+13)Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ed0242a-2db9-5144-8e11-5ba6887b8361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9575d503-b33a-5aaf-a793-2497c79dc90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{-5(t-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769} :answer-field/id #uuid "5479ca0e-e235-5daf-9f78-3a7ac798d247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0f1706f-269a-5658-8fd3-75993082b4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345300" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+6{y}^{′}(t)+5y(t)=20{u}_{4}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s)=\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+5}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=4,$ $B=-5,$ and $C=1,$ giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5},\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nf(t)=4-5{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-4s}F(s)\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{4}(t)\\,f(t-4) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}+{e}^{-5(t-4)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99b6edeb-bbfc-5c8b-b1c4-dd5efc3764aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a207cf-6ec4-5e6d-997d-39d88a243b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4db7a8-b2f3-5998-9cda-8fbb2aabb05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517567} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf6de9-76a1-580c-a0a5-04e50833b026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3s+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517752} {:answer/id #uuid "8f14cb64-f15c-5e63-97dc-cb8d52fe503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517757} {:answer/id #uuid "a5df1187-9902-5a00-900d-f4f5ec5cd0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{s({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d0813c-5c54-5890-9610-b5222646eaaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2f59173-bdb2-59d3-8bec-268e720e4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345246" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{4}(t)=u(t-4)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+16y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=4$ and $f(t)=7(t-4).$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{7u(t-4)\\cdot (t-4)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7((t+4)-4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7t\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)(t-4)\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-3+16Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+16)Y(s) & =3+\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b7f1db-a884-5b7a-8f66-cefcb30e2b44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462f1b1b-9444-58ff-b801-8fbbfcbcb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377461} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9218e-9c07-5797-9e3d-2114ea136be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377462} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30168a-f94d-52a2-9a98-bf5ae26c54ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377463} {:answer/id #uuid "5d96461b-b5bf-5858-b6b1-4e9dc9828ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} {:answer/id #uuid "b67556e4-5189-5cc7-aaee-81b6db402e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4669c7-fc85-5f41-a5f5-f8f6933fbb16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e512d666-26fd-5562-9dad-5a955b3c727f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345242" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+9y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5(t-3)^{2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u(t-3)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5((t+3)-3)^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)(t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)+9Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+9)Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9df5258-7677-5050-9ab9-8650f43b3255" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35b6c1e-f747-5862-8863-fe053c5aa6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377482} {:answer/id #uuid "143176f2-5f66-5c47-bc95-252c5655a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\cos(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c74efd7-0f5a-5f39-afee-3b02924def1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3731bc55-d35c-5b26-9516-425a707e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-345263" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)={u}_{1}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-1)\\,\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1})\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{1}(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-1)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb271466-d784-5171-b57b-f15347d64110" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277}}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517488}]}
